WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.274 --> 00:00:09.123 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Welcome to Japan's prefectures, part 1 of 4. First, I clear the chosen names
for a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:09.773 --> 00:00:21.162 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
We work through 7 of the Japan regions the map names, once each: where it
sits, one fact worth keeping, and a hook to remember it by.

3
00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:24.328 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Hokkaidō into view.

4
00:00:28.691 --> 00:00:40.765 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Hokkaidō is Japan’s northern island, lying north of Honshū across the Tsugaru
Strait, with Sapporo in its southern plain and no land border with any other
prefecture.

5
00:00:41.426 --> 00:00:52.432 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its name was only settled in 1869, when the old word Ezo, the land of the
Ainu, was replaced by a phrase meaning north sea circuit.

6
00:00:53.085 --> 00:01:02.164 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the northern island that reaches up out of the monsoon belt, with
Sapporo’s snow slides and the potato fields.

7
00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:05.716 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Aomori into view.

8
00:01:09.457 --> 00:01:20.881 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Aomori stands at the northern tip of Honshū on the Sea of Japan, with Iwate
below it and Akita to the south-west, and the city of Aomori on its bay.

9
00:01:21.539 --> 00:01:33.869 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The Sannai-Maruyama site here was a settled village 5,900 years ago, which
showed that the Jōmon people farmed and built rather than only foraging.

10
00:01:34.529 --> 00:01:43.701 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the blue forest at the top of the island, where half of Japan’s
apples grow and house-sized paper warriors parade each August.

11
00:01:44.376 --> 00:01:47.104 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Akita into view.

12
00:01:50.807 --> 00:02:03.520 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Akita faces the Sea of Japan on the north-west of Honshū, below Aomori and
beside Iwate, with Miyagi and Yamagata to the south and the city of Akita on
the shore.

13
00:02:04.177 --> 00:02:14.278 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It is the home of the Akita dog, and Hachikō, one of them, waited nine years
at Shibuya station in Tokyo for a master who never came back.

14
00:02:14.927 --> 00:02:22.810 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the autumn field on the grey northern coast, the dog that waited, and
the rice paddies with the highest yield in the country.

15
00:02:23.491 --> 00:02:25.360 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Iwate into view.

16
00:02:30.286 --> 00:02:41.165 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Iwate faces the Pacific on the north-east of Honshū, with Aomori above it and
Akita and Miyagi to the west and south, and Morioka a little way inland.

17
00:02:41.825 --> 00:02:59.645 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Miyazawa Kenji, the poet whose verse every Japanese schoolchild learns, wrote
about these hills and fields, and the Sanriku coast here was the worst hit by
the 2011 tsunami, which reached over 30 metres in places.

18
00:03:00.296 --> 00:03:08.051 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the boulder with the demon’s handprint and the long curve of the
Sanriku coast where the water reached thirty metres.

19
00:03:08.721 --> 00:03:10.973 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Yamagata into view.

20
00:03:15.205 --> 00:03:26.374 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Yamagata lies inland in the north of Honshū in a ring of peaks around its
basin, with Akita to the north and Miyagi to the east, and Fukushima and
Niigata to the south.

21
00:03:27.038 --> 00:03:34.584 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It grows about 70% of Japan’s cherries, and its Yonezawa beef ranks with
Kobe’s.

22
00:03:35.245 --> 00:03:42.791 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the three sacred mountains of Dewa and the wooden street of Ginzan
hot spring under the snow.

23
00:03:43.496 --> 00:03:45.714 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Miyagi into view.

24
00:03:50.990 --> 00:04:02.461 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Miyagi faces the Pacific on the east of Honshū, below Iwate and above
Fukushima, with Sendai, the largest city in northern Honshū, on the bay.

25
00:04:03.116 --> 00:04:14.668 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The bay off it, Matsushima, with its 260 pine islands, is one of the three
classic views of Japan, and Sendai was built by Date Masamune, the one-eyed
warlord.

26
00:04:15.321 --> 00:04:22.821 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the bay of 260 pine islands and the one-eyed warlord’s castle city on
the north-east coast.

27
00:04:23.574 --> 00:04:26.302 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Fukushima into view.

28
00:04:31.285 --> 00:04:42.071 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Fukushima faces the Pacific on the east of Honshū, with Miyagi and Yamagata to
the north, and Iwaki on its coast with Gunma, Tochigi and Ibaraki inland
behind it.

29
00:04:42.735 --> 00:04:53.056 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its Aizu region held out for the shogun against the imperial army in 1868, and
the story of the teenage Byakkotai is still taught in Japanese schools.

30
00:04:53.721 --> 00:05:00.327 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the fortune island with the castle on the rise in the marsh, and the
boy soldiers who held the snowed-in fortress.

31
00:05:06.506 --> 00:05:20.450 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
7 regions of Japan, one for each target of the map: Hokkaidō, Aomori, Akita,
Iwate, Yamagata, Miyagi and Fukushima.

32
00:05:21.105 --> 00:05:31.937 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the shape of Japan from the inside: the regions nest around each
other, and every name on the map belongs somewhere in this one country.
